// CM33 value platform + THREE independent NETC wire ports on point-to-point UDP.
//
// imxrt1180-netc-flood asserts that a broadcast ingressing wire 1 floods out BOTH 0
// and 2 and is suppressed on its own ingress port. That is only expressible if the
// three ports are INDEPENDENTLY OBSERVABLE, so each is its own connectable node
// rather than the block's single IMACInterface.
//
// Socket layout mirrors @rt1180emulator's run.sh exactly, so their flood.py /
// portfwd.py drive this unchanged:
//   port 0: emulator receives on 44002, sends egress to the harness on 44001
//   port 1: receives on 44012, sends to 44011   (the injection wire)
//   port 2: receives on 44022, sends to 44021
//
// ⚠ POINT-TO-POINT, NEVER SHARED. A switch flooding onto a reflective segment
// re-ingests its own flood and storms -- a real loop, but not the topology under
// test, and it would read as a forwarding defect while being a backend artefact.
// EchoWire (shared, reflective) is for netc-rxfwd's self-echo oracle only.
using "mimxrt1189_cm33_value.repl"

netc_port0: Network.IMXRT1180_NETC_Port @ sysbus 0x71001000
    portIndex: 0
    Switch: netc

netc_port1: Network.IMXRT1180_NETC_Port @ sysbus 0x71002000
    portIndex: 1
    Switch: netc

netc_port2: Network.IMXRT1180_NETC_Port @ sysbus 0x71003000
    portIndex: 2
    Switch: netc

udpwire0: Network.UdpWire @ sysbus 0x71011000
    listenPort: 44002
    remotePort: 44001

udpwire1: Network.UdpWire @ sysbus 0x71012000
    listenPort: 44012
    remotePort: 44011

udpwire2: Network.UdpWire @ sysbus 0x71013000
    listenPort: 44022
    remotePort: 44021
